@@ -2,6 +2,16 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Developer diagnostics connections
+
+The homepage includes independent browser connections to a hardware test device on the reader’s LAN and a companion CLI on the reader’s machine. The editable example endpoints are `http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/status` and `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health`; these are placeholders, not bundled services. Supply the actual device and CLI status endpoints. LAN targets accept private IP literals and `.local` hostnames; CLI targets accept loopback addresses and localhost.
+
+Each Connect click makes one direct, credential-free CORS GET request and shows the successful response as a timestamped snapshot. No scanning, polling, server proxy, or page-load connection requests are used. Endpoints must return a successful HTTP status and standard `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers allowing the website’s origin (including the port, excluding its path). JSON, plain text, and empty successful responses are supported; displayed responses are limited to 4,000 characters.
+
+Serve the website over HTTPS. Each card queries its granular `local-network` or `loopback-network` permission, watches for permission changes, and explains how to unblock denied access in browser site settings. Unsupported permission queries fall back to explicit user-triggered attempts. Requests specify the corresponding `targetAddressSpace`. Browsers without local HTTP support may need a trusted HTTPS endpoint. No deprecated Private Network Access preflight headers are required. See [Chrome’s Local Network Access guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/local-network-access).
+
+Run the network helper tests without starting services: `node --test tests/diagnostics.test.mjs` (Node 22.18+ or Node 24).
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
